d there is certainly a feeling that a  significant number of those who have  been coming here have been coming here  for for criminal reasons well you know  Albania's prime minister ready Ram is  not desperately happy about this he  tweeted this last night Albania is not a  rich country and was for a very long  time a victim of Empires we never had  our own we have a duty to fight crime at  home and are doing so resolutely as  cooperating closely with others too  ready to work closer with the UK but but  facts are crucial  um so is mutual regret I think we may  have the wrong one there but in essence  what what the Albanian prime minister is  suggesting is that perhaps some of the  rhetoric that is being directed at his  fellow countrymen and women might just  be slightly over the top well I'm  grateful for the um uh the work we're  doing with the albanians and they have  been sending senior police  officers over so as to speed up  processing we've got a expedited system  to return albanians if it turns out that  they are not legitimate Asylum Seekers  so we are trying to work in cooperation  but clearly there's pressure you can see  it from the councils you can see it from  uh members of parliament you can hear it  from people people and journalists  around the country it can lead to  language which is sometimes unfortunate  and it is worth putting on record our  gratitude to the Albanian government for  their cooperation  um in working to sort the sound let's  let as as promised move on to your brief  How concerned were you uh by the UN  emissions Gap report that emerged  yesterday  um well we're absolutely focused on  making sure that the Glasgow climate  pact is converted into action  um and as you say the world is not on  track for 1.5 degrees  um  2.8 degrees report after report after  report  um and that is why I mean why have we  taken the actions we've taken we've cut  our emissions in this country  domestically we're only a small part of  the globe but set